{"step_index":1991,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-03T08:33:03Z","input_tokens":2593,"cache_read_tokens":214274,"output_tokens":3758,"thinking":"I'm thinking through how to approach this.\n\n\nExamining the new UI element, the focus is on integrating the \"Save Bill to Tax Ledger\" button. This involves linking the button's click event to a dedicated `onSaveToLedger` function. Proceeding to a more detailed analysis of the Tax & ITR Filing tab's functionality.\n\n\nThe UI design focuses on three key areas: a bill table displaying saved bills with aggregated totals, an aggregated GST ITC ledger for GSTR-3B preparation with compliance checks, and a customizable annual business and ITR calculator. The calculator allows for switching between presumptive income (Sec 44AD) and normal profit & loss modes. The system will dynamically calculate net profit and tax liability based on user inputs and saved bill data.\n\n\nThe system needs to store bill data in local storage with a specific structure. The structure includes an ID, date, invoice number, vendor name, GSTIN, and various financial fields like subtotal, CGST, SGST, IGST, and total values. This data will be saved under the key `'audit_it_tax_ledger_bills'`.\n\n\nThe bill saving logic checks for existing invoices by number, updating if a match is found; otherwise, it adds the new bill. This process occurs when a user triggers the save action. If no existing bills are found in local storage, a default sample bill might be necessary. It will be helpful to offer a streamlined method for adding the current verified bill when no bills exist, or when the user loads a sample bill.\n\n\nThe \"Tax & ITR Filing\" tab requires refactoring the existing component into a comprehensive ledger. This will involve rendering a table of stored bills, aggregated totals, and GST ITC information. The table will contain columns for bill details, with action buttons to view and delete entries.\n\n\nThe system will include a compliance reminder regarding the 180-day rule for vendor payments to avoid Input Tax Credit (ITC) reversal. It will also calculate net cash GST outflow, subtracting accumulated eligible ITC from gross output GST collected. The annual business and ITR calculator provides an input for annual gross sales/turnover and allows users to switch between presumptive income (Sec 44AD) and normal profit & loss modes.\n\n\nThe tax liability calculation requires implementing the new tax regime's slabs and applying the standard deduction. Section 87A rebate applicability hinges on taxable income, displaying a zero-tax message if it's below ₹7,00,000.  Audit applicability will also need to be checked based on turnover and digital transaction percentage for both presumptive and normal profit modes. The system needs to export the tax summary and ledger bills to CSV or PDF.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"read_file","args":{"EndLine":170,"Path":"/Users/pranit405/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/audit-it/components/ReviewWorkspace.js","StartLine":125}}]}
